/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Gratitude should first be of a heart posture, which is enriched with the knowledge of God's goodness and faithfulness across generations; a heart full of meditations on the mighty demonstration of God's power in and through the lives of His faithful servants, in the holy scriptures. Then, there should be an outflow of gratitude from such heart through the fruits of one's lips. Heb. 10:15 "By him therefore let us offer the sacrifice of praise to God continually, that is, the fruit of our lips giving thanks to his name." Gratitude is not received if it is not communicated, a grateful person must give a literal expression of his thankfulness to God, through words, songs, shouts, claps, dances and even through instruments of praise. God is delighted and pleased in a child who knows of the gracefulness of His father, and is generous to give Him praise, come what may.)
